WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:03.080
Movie-making isn't an exact science.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:07.050
From the biggest blockbuster to the cheapest
"Z movie," filmmakers all start out with a

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:10.349
desire to entertain, but many times it doesn't
go as planned.

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:14.119
The pieces just fail to come together, and
we're left with a product that is less than

00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:15.119
transcendent.

00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:19.130
However, there are also times when those dysfunctional
pieces come together perfectly, and a film

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:24.970
project transcends badness to become something
truly, jaw-droppingly, spectacularly awful.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:26.669
Here are some of those films.

00:00:26.669 --> 00:00:29.039
Manos: The Hands of Fate

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:33.239
Hal Warren was a Texas fertilizer salesman
who happened to make the acquaintance of Academy

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:38.329
Award-winning screenwriter Stirling Silliphant
while working as an extra on a TV show.

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:41.970
According to Entertainment Weekly, Warren
proclaimed to his friend that filmmaking was

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.879
easy, and ended up betting that he could make
and star in a movie himself.

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:50.129
He wrote the first treatment right there on
a cocktail napkin, and one of the most ill-conceived

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:54.079
ideas in cinematic history, Manos: The Hands
of Fate, was born.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:58.180
The film that resulted from the friendly bet
has so many problems that it'd be easier to

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.570
catalog what didn't go wrong.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:05.470
Shot for $19,000, the Z-horror picture, in
which a vacationing family stumbles across

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:10.960
a weird cult, features long, dialogue-free
shots of people driving, reaction shots that

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.960
don't appear to be reactions to anything,
dead-end subplots, shots that inexplicably

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.390
linger on after characters have stopped talking…

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:21.360
"There's no place like that around here…"

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:33.120
And overall, some of the most poorly-delivered,
atrociously written dialogue you will ever

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:34.130
hear.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:35.130
"That's ten miles…"

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.130
"Ten miles?

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:39.729
Might as well be ten thousand miles."

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:44.049
Its insanely shoddy production values have
become the stuff of legend, especially after

00:01:44.049 --> 00:01:47.340
the film's notorious roasting on Mystery Science
Theater 3000.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.700
"Mike!

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:49.860
Help us!"

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.399
"You have to want to help yourself."

00:01:52.399 --> 00:01:57.119
The film was lovingly restored by a fan in
2011, so its astonishing ineptitude can be

00:01:57.119 --> 00:02:01.049
revisited by generations of future filmmakers.

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:02.649
Troll 2

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:08.009
The problems with Troll 2 begin with the fact
that it is not, in fact, a sequel to 1986's

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:11.630
Troll, and the villains are goblins, not trolls.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:16.220
According to Film School Rejects, the film
has become renowned for its incredibly clunky,

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.940
heavy-handed, needlessly expository dialogue,
which was the result of its screenplay being

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.310
written by a non-English speaker, Italian
writer/director Claudio Fragasso.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.030
"They're eating her.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:31.030
And then they're going to eat me."

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:35.870
Despite appeals from his amateur cast, Fragasso
insisted they deliver each line exactly as

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:40.500
written, and their enthusiastic overacting
renders nearly every line of dialogue in Troll

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.500
2 borderline excruciating.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.780
"Oh my God what's happening to her?

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.140
And why can't I move?

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:47.940
There must be a logical reason for all of
this."

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:48.940
"Shut up!"

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.810
The cinematography and special effects are
appropriately amateurish, the loopy plot fails

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:58.320
to ever follow through on its occasional threat
to make sense, and its scenes of "horror"

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.160
register as explicitly comical.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:06.280
"Oh my Godddd!"

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.140
Actor Michael Stephenson went on to direct
the 2009 documentary Best Worst Movie, detailing

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:16.871
his experience working on Troll 2, which contains
the depressing revelation that Fragasso, for

00:03:16.871 --> 00:03:20.710
all these years, was under the impression
that he'd made an awesome movie.

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.840
"I did a very good movie.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:30.100
If the others say worst movie, them problem."

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:34.060
"Oh my gosh, that is the worst movie I've
ever seen."

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:35.510
MAC and Me

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:40.831
The 1988 film MAC and Me was an attempt, about
five or six years too late, to cash in on

00:03:40.831 --> 00:03:45.400
the box-office-breaking success of Steven
Spielberg's E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial, but

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.620
that's not all it was.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.730
It was also an attempt to further cash in
by stuffing the film to the brim with arguably

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.540
the most name-brand products ever before seen
on-screen.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.760
In the movie, a young, wheelchair-bound boy
befriends a horrifyingly ugly animatronic

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:03.670
alien, and the pair awkwardly stumble through
a story that only pauses long enough to prominently

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.480
feature corporate logos.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:09.870
MAC and Me is a film that can't be bothered
to present a coherent plot, yet has no trouble

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:14.970
grinding the proceedings to a halt for a full-on,
music video-style dance number set entirely

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:20.010
inside of a McDonald's restaurant, and the
friendly alien's name was surely no coincidence.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:24.150
The dialogue is overwhelmingly cheesy, the
alien looks like it could have come from your

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:29.070
neighborhood Halloween store, and the plot's
lazy reworking of E.T. is inane even by the

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:32.850
standards of cheaply produced '80s kids' movies.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.620
Howard the Duck

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.411
It's tough to remember when Marvel didn't
utterly dominate the box office, but there

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:42.380
was a time when Hollywood simply had no idea
how to handle their properties.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:46.380
For the strongest evidence of Tinseltown's
comic cluelessness, look no further than the

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.690
very first big-screen adaptation of a Marvel
property.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:54.990
To call 1986's Howard the Duck a starring
vehicle for a C-list character would be generous.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:58.600
But producer George Lucas apparently felt
he could do no wrong in the mid-'80s, and

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:04.090
he brought the full staff of his company Industrial
Light and Magic and a $30 million budget to

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:08.160
the production, which would almost instantly
be pegged as one of the biggest flops of its

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:09.160
decade.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:13.620
The film isn't so much funny as it is deeply
weird, throwing Howard, who is mysteriously

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:17.980
transported to Earth from his native Duckworld,
together with human love interest Beverly,

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:19.310
played by Lea Thompson.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:23.490
It's a strange pairing in the comics that's
rendered downright disturbing in live-action.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:28.180
Its tone-deaf attempts at humor and creepy
animatronics utterly baffled audiences.

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.260
"Howard, wait!..."

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:31.300
"AAHH!"

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:33.840
"Uh-huh, typical hairless ape."

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:37.000
The film struggled to make back half its budget
domestically.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.100
It showed the first big chinks in Lucas' armor,
and tried its best to ruin a character who

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.960
wouldn't appear on film again for nearly 30
years.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:48.850
"You know, this is beginning to seriously
undermine my self-esteem."

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:51.490
Birdemic: Shock and Terror

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:55.720
If you gathered together a group of random
people with absolutely no interest in film

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:00.690
and forced them at gunpoint to make a horror
feature with almost no money and fewer resources,

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:04.420
you would end up with with something that
would look a whole lot like 2008's Birdemic:

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:05.460
Shock and Terror.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:09.510
This complete ripoff of Alfred Hitchcock's
The Birds tells the simple story of a happy

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:10.560
couple…

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:11.620
"Wow.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:12.680
Congratulations.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:14.150
I think you'll look great in those lingerie."

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:19.220
...whose romance is interrupted by an unexplained
killer bird infestation.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:23.880
Its overall aesthetic, shot on the cheap,
stiffly acted, and incompetently plotted despite

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:27.820
the story's simplicity, would've been more
than enough to earn the film its place among

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.220
the worst of the worst.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.410
But the birds themselves simply must be seen
to be believed, rendered with the absolute

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:38.980
cheapest CGI anyone has ever seen, dropped
clumsily into the frame as our heroes swat

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:40.710
at the squawking menaces.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:44.639
The repetitive, grating sound effects that
accompany their attacks just make the "terror"

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.480
seem even more ridiculous.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:59.430
Some observers have gone so far as to wonder
whether Birdemic was actually intended to

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:00.430
be funny.

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:02.970
According to director James Nguyen, it wasn't.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:07.180
"It's shocking and terrifying, just like a
100 million dollar picture, Hollywood style."

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:09.120
"You think this looks like a 100 million dollar
picture?"

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.240
"Well, I think from From a distance, you know?"

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:12.400
"From a distance?"

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:14.020
Movie 43

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:18.980
The cringeworthy Movie 43 is the result of
an unearthly amount of determination on the

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:23.550
part of Peter Farrelly, once best-known as
the mastermind behind gross-out comedy classic

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:24.870
There's Something About Mary.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:29.000
Farrelly envisioned a new breed of sketch
comedy, a film that would play like a series

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.780
of Funny or Die skits, but… grosser.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:35.840
According to The Guardian, Farrelly adopted
a strategy of waiting, sometimes for years,

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:41.191
to shoot segments based on his stars' availability,
and even moved the production 3,000 miles

00:07:41.191 --> 00:07:43.200
to accommodate Richard Gere.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:47.280
Through sheer patience, pestering and more
than a little guilt, he managed to enlist

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.010
major Hollywood heavy hitters to completely
embarrass themselves in service of the most

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:55.930
horrifyingly unfunny so-called comedy ever
produced.

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:59.580
It's been ripped to shreds by critics, but
it was famed critic Richard Roeper who hit

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.480
the nail on the head when he said,

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.310
"[Movie 43] is the Citizen Kane of awful."

